================================================================================
                    DESCRIPTION FOR THESIS PAPER
================================================================================

The FireWire system operates using an Input-Process-Output (IPO) framework, 
which is a standard model for describing information systems and IoT applications.

INPUT STAGE:
The input stage comprises three categories of data sources. First, hardware 
sensors continuously collect environmental data: the MQ-2 sensor detects smoke 
and combustible gases (LPG, methane, propane); the MQ-7 sensor measures carbon 
monoxide concentration in parts per million (PPM); the MQ-135 sensor monitors 
air quality index (AQI); and the HDC1080 sensor tracks temperature and humidity 
levels. Second, user inputs include authentication credentials (PIN code, Google 
OAuth login), household identification (Household ID, Access Code), and manual 
calibration settings via physical potentiometers. Third, connectivity inputs 
consist of WiFi network credentials enabling the ESP32 to communicate with the 
cloud backend.

PROCESS STAGE:
The process stage involves two primary processing units operating in tandem. 
The ESP32 microcontroller performs local processing by continuously sampling 
analog sensor values, applying digital filtering to reduce noise, converting 
raw voltage readings to meaningful units (percentage, PPM, degrees Celsius), 
and comparing these values against user-defined thresholds. When a threshold 
is exceeded, the ESP32 immediately activates local alarm outputs and prepares 
data packets for transmission to the cloud server. The Node.js backend server 
handles cloud processing by authenticating incoming requests, validating and 
processing sensor data, storing readings in the MongoDB database, evaluating 
alert conditions, and triggering notification services when alarms are detected.

OUTPUT STAGE:
The output stage delivers information through three channels. Local outputs 
provide immediate on-site alerts through the 20×4 LCD display showing real-time 
sensor values and status messages, and the piezoelectric buzzer emitting audible 
alarms. These local outputs function even during internet outages, ensuring 
continuous protection. Remote outputs enable off-site monitoring through push 
notifications sent to registered smartphones and real-time updates on the web 
dashboard accessible from any internet-enabled device. Data outputs include 
timestamped sensor logs and alarm history records stored in MongoDB, which can 
be reviewed for post-event analysis or exported as PDF reports.

The system operates in a continuous monitoring loop, returning to the input 
stage after each processing cycle to maintain real-time hazard detection. This 
IPO framework ensures that FireWire provides comprehensive fire and gas 
monitoring through the integration of local and cloud-based components.


